'''''Trifolium ochroleucon''''', also known as '''''Trifolium ochroleucum'''''<ref>{{cite web |title=Sulphur Clover |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/52266/i-Trifolium-ochroleucon-i/Details |website=RHS |accessdate=6 May 2020}}</ref> or '''sulphur clover''', is a [[perennial]] species of [[clover]] in the family [[Fabaceae]].

== Name ==
The genus name, ''[[clover|Trifolium]]'', derives from the Latin ''{{lang|la|[[:wikt:tres|tres]]}}'', "three", and ''{{lang|la|[[:wikt:folium|folium]]}}'', "leaf", so called from the characteristic form of the leaf, which usually but not always has three [[leaflet (botany)|leaflets]] ([[trifoliolate]]); hence the popular name "[[trefoil]]". The species name, ''ochroleucon'', is Latin for "yellowish-white", referring to the colour of the flowers.

==Distribution and habitat==
The plant is native to Europe.<ref>{{cite web |title=Sulphur Clover |url=https://www.plantlife.org.uk/uk/discover-wild-plants-nature/plant-fungi-species/sulphur-clover |website=Plantlife |accessdate=6 May 2020 |archive-date=11 July 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190711180757/https://www.plantlife.org.uk/uk/discover-wild-plants-nature/plant-fungi-species/sulphur-clover |url-status=dead }}</ref> It is localised in the British Isles, with the main stronghold of the species being in [[East Anglia]], whilst the species is also present in [[Lincolnshire]], [[Merseyside]] and [[Worcestershire]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Trifolium ochroleucon |url=https://species.nbnatlas.org/species/NHMSYS0000464580 |website=NBN Atlas |accessdate=6 May 2020}}</ref> It is fairly widespread throughout the rest of Western and Central Europe, and it has also been recorded from [[Iran]] and North Africa.<ref>{{cite web |title=Trifolium ochroleucon |url=http://www.brc.ac.uk/plantatlas/plant/trifolium-ochroleucon |website=Online Atlas of the British and Irish Fauna |publisher=BRC |accessdate=6 May 2020 }}{{Dead link|date=December 2024 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

It can be found in grassy places, predominantly on clay soils.<ref>Complete Guide to British Flowers, First Edition, Collins, 2006.</ref> 

